  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Leveraging Best Practices for Upgrading the Mulberry Silk Value Chain in Ethiopia through AI and Digital Tools</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ethiopia possesses strong potential for expanding its sericulture industry, with pilot sites confirming favorable conditions for mulberry cultivation and silkworm rearing. Despite this promise, current activities remain fragmented, small-scale, and constrained by limited technical capacity, outdated processing technologies, and weak institutional coordination. Farmers lack improved mulberry varieties, climate-adapted silkworm breeds, and standardized disease-control measures, while processing enterprises rely on manual reeling methods that restrict productivity and quality. Market linkages are underdeveloped, and value-added production is minimal, preventing Ethiopian silk from achieving competitiveness in international markets. By contrast, Hechi City in China represents the global benchmark for mulberry silk production, with centuries of expertise, standardized cultivation, advanced disease-control systems, semi-automated reeling facilities, and digital applications in precision agriculture and quality management. Previous cooperation between Hechi and Ethiopia#including training programs and the introduction of drought-resistant silkworm varieties#provides a strong foundation for scaling Ethiopia#s sericulture sector. The baseline highlights both challenges and opportunities. At the production level, Ethiopi</narrative>
  </description>
